# wiki_pending_baseline_vs_concurrent_ingest: the manual "Ingest now"
# and the launchd tick meeting at .ingest.lock, checked at the level of
# what pending.go and the marker in log.md actually record — not the
# lock file itself (wiki_check_race, wiki_edit_vs_ingest_lock cover
# that) but whether the range a run commits for a session is the range
# it read, once two starters have gone through the lock in some order.
#
# Product:
#   go/plugins/wiki/run.go Run: tryLock(.ingest.lock) first (a second
#     starter that finds it held returns nil at once and touches nothing
#     — wiki_edit_vs_ingest_lock's OneRunHoldsTheLock); only the holder
#     calls FindPending, so the read of log.md a run acts on is always
#     taken *after* the lock, never before it.
#   go/plugins/wiki/pending.go FindPending/readLog: `done[id]` is the
#     max seq over every "## [...] ingest | id#seq | ..." heading in
#     log.md, so an out-of-order append can never make done[id] regress
#     — this is the thing this spec exists to pin down, because a naive
#     "last heading wins" reading of log.md would let a stale write
#     silently undo a later one's ground.
#   go/plugins/wiki/SKILL.md (the ingest agent, run headless inside
#     Run): the heading it appends names "the last seq in the digest it
#     built" — the range it actually read at its own start, not
#     whatever the session has grown to by the time it writes. New
#     conversation entries a person adds to S while a run holds the
#     lock are simply left pending for the next tick.
#
# The world: one session S with one ever-growing entries counter and
# one lock two Runner instances (Manual = the person's "Ingest now",
# Scheduler = launchd's tick) contend for. A run that gets the lock
# reads S's entries as `from`..`to` (a snapshot), and its commit records
# exactly that `to`, never something read later.
#
# Abstractions:
# - Only one session, so "which session" never has to be modelled: the
#   only thing worth checking is whether the recorded range for S ever
#   loses or fabricates ground.
# - The agent's own work (writing pages, deciding a disposition) is not
#   modelled: Commit is the single step from "holds the lock, has read
#   a range" to "log.md has a heading for it and the lock is free".
# - A run that finds nothing pending (to == from) still takes and
#   releases the lock in the product; skipping that state here would
#   lose no reachable ground so it is left out to keep the graph small.
#
# Fairness: NewEntry, Lock and Commit are unfair (a person may never
# type again, a tick may never come, a run may run forever); nothing
# here needs a liveness claim about the log ever catching up.

role Runner:
    action Init:
        self.phase = "idle"      # idle -> locked -> committed -> idle
        self.lo = 0
        self.hi = 0

    # Take the lock and read pending: FindPending's snapshot of S.
    atomic action Lock:
        require self.phase == "idle" and lock == "free"
        lock = "held"
        self.phase = "locked"
        self.lo = committed
        self.hi = entries

    # The agent runs and writes the heading for exactly [from, to).
    atomic action Commit:
        require self.phase == "locked"
        if self.hi >= committed:
            committed = self.hi
        else:
            regressed = True
        self.phase = "idle"
        lock = "free"

action Init:
    lock = "free"
    entries = 0
    committed = 0
    regressed = False
    runner0 = Runner()
    runner1 = Runner()

# A person or something else adds a conversation entry to S at any time,
# including while a run holds the lock. Capped at 2: one entry ahead of
# whatever a run has read is enough to show a run's own snapshot never
# moves out from under it; unbounded growth is not a new state, just a
# bigger number, and would make the graph infinite.
atomic action NewEntry:
    require entries < 2
    entries = entries + 1

# At most one runner holds the lock.
always assertion OneRunnerLocked:
    return not (runner0.phase == "locked" and runner1.phase == "locked")

# A commit never records less ground than log.md already has: the
# thing a naive "last heading wins" reading would get wrong.
always assertion CommittedNeverRegresses:
    return not regressed

# What is committed was, at some point, actually read: it never runs
# ahead of the entries that exist.
always assertion CommittedNeverExceedsEntries:
    return committed <= entries

# The lock is free exactly when no runner holds it.
always assertion LockMatchesHolders:
    return (lock == "held") == (runner0.phase == "locked" or runner1.phase == "locked")
